Large language models exhibit biases toward familiar statements and patterns from their training data. While biases may be useful heuristics for producing plausible text in some settings, they can undermine the models' ability to reason in an objective, logical manner.

_Contextual calibration_ is a method for reducing the influence of these biases in settings like multiple choice tests, where models give short answers without reasoning. We adapt the idea of contextual calibration to multi-step reasoning traces. Our new method called _context relevance_ quantifies how much likelihood a model attributes to a reasoning step because of its specific context as opposed to the model's internal bias.

We evaluate context relevance as an indicator for reasoning correctness in three different datasets (MR-GSM8K, BIG-Bench Mistake, and ProcessBench) containing annotated solutions to mathematical and logical questions. We compute the per-step context relevance for all 8,584 reasoning traces from the datasets using five different language models from the Qwen and Llama families. Pooling steps within each dataset, fully ablated context relevance is associated with reasoning errors across all five models (AUROC 0.645–0.751 for negative context relevance). In one task category, question-ablated context relevance reaches an AUROC of 0.904.

As a baseline, we evaluate the models' ability to find reasoning errors when explicitly prompted to do so (_LLM judge_). Qwen3.5-27B is the strongest judge we evaluated. Its reported scores exceed some published GPT-4 results, although differences in prompts and evaluation protocols limit direct comparison.

Despite the association between low context relevance and reasoning errors, the stronger LLM judges locate errors more accurately than the context relevance signal alone. Across the three benchmarks, Qwen3.5-27B achieves 43.4–72.1% exact first-error-or-NONE accuracy as a judge, compared with 21.9–47.7% for a classifier based on its fully ablated context relevance.
